bool stable_flight(std::vector<int> q, int w, int k) {
    // Check whether q is a palindrome
    if (!std::equal(q.begin(), q.begin() + q.size()/2, q.rbegin())) {
        return false;
    }
    // Check whether sum of elements in q doesn't exceed w
    else if (std::accumulate(q.begin(), q.end(), 0) > w) {
        return false;
    }
    // Check whether no element in q exceeds k
    else if (*std::max_element(q.begin(), q.end()) > k) {
        return false;
    }
    // If q satisfies all conditions, return True
    else {
        return true;
    }
}
```

Explanation of what's going on in the C++ code:

1. For the palindrome check, `std::equal()` is a function that checks if two ranges in a collection are equal. The function here checks if the first half of `q` is the same as the reversed second half.

2. `std::accumulate()` is a function that returns the sum of the elements in the range `[q.begin(), q.end())`. It's being used here to check if the sum of the elements in `q` is greater than `w`.

3. The `std::max_element()` function returns an iterator pointing to the maximum element in the range `[q.begin(), q.end())`. We deference this iterator to get the max value and compare it with `k`.

Note: In C++, arrays and other container types do not have built-in support for operations like 'sum' or 'any' as in Python, so the STL provides functions like `std::accumulate()` and `std::max_element()` for these purposes.

