```perl
# Define the subroutine
sub decode_complex_colors {
    my ($color_string) = @_; # Get the parameter

    # Create a hash that maps each hex digit to its integer value
    my %hex_to_int = map { $_ => $_ * 15 } 0..9;
    my @hex_chars = split //, 'ABCDEFabcdef';
    @hex_to_int{@hex_chars} = (map { $_ * 15 } 10..15), (0..5);

    # Split the color string into individual colors
    my @colors = split / /, $color_string;

    # Initialize array to hold RGB values
    my @rgb_values;

    # Loop over each color
    for my $color (@colors) {
        # Check if the color is valid
        if (length($color) != 4 || substr($color, 0, 1) ne '#' || grep { not exists $hex_to_int{$_} } split //, substr($color, 1)) {
            die "Invalid color code";
        }
        # Convert each color to RGB values and add it to the array
        push @rgb_values, [map {$hex_to_int{$_}} split //, substr($color,1)];
    }

    # Return the array of RGB values
    return @rgb_values;
}
```

This Perl script does the same thing as the Python script: 
- It first creates a map `hex_to_int`, where the keys are hexadecimal digit characters, and the values are their integer values times 15 (for 0-9 and A-F) or just their integer values (for a-f).
- Next, it splits the input string `color_string` by whitespace to get the individual colors, and initializes an empty array `rgb_values` to store the RGB values of the colors. 
